{"id": 91, "ts": 1784170145.957254, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I acknowledge Markus\u2019s point that Candidate\u202fC brings strong situational\u2011awareness abilities\u2014anticipating danger, seeing complex connections, and excellent spatial vision\u2014plus solid leadership qualities. Those are indeed valuable for long\u2011distance ops. However, the gaps we still have on C are exactly the same safety\u2011critical gaps we face with Candidate\u202fB: we have no concrete evidence of C\u2019s decision quality under pressure, his adaptability to feedback, or how his \u201cunfriendly\u201d and \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d demeanor translates into crew cooperation and professional communication.\nFrom the data we do have on B, he is **very conscientious**, handles stress very well, and assesses weather conditions effectively\u2014core reliability and stress\u2011resilience factors for international routes. The only crew\u2011cooperation note we received is that he can be \u201cgrumpy and uncooperative,\u201d which is a concern but still less severe than a documented incident of unprofessional remarks (the pretentious tone). Moreover, we lack any direct observations of B\u2019s decision\u2011making in crises, responsibility on high\u2011risk flights, or attention to operational details, just as we lack them for C.\nGiven that both candidates have comparable evidence gaps, the differentiator is the **proven reliability** and **stress handling** that B already demonstrates, which are non\u2011negotiable for long\u2011distance safety. Until we obtain concrete evidence that C\u2019s technical strengths translate into superior crisis decisions and crew harmony, I will keep my provisional vote for **Candidate\u202fB**."}
